# Classes Can Also Have Methods

Right now your classes only store attributes.

But later you’ll add behavior.

Example:

```ruby id="xmlns14"
class BankAccount
  def initialize(balance)
    @balance = balance
  end

  def deposit(amount)
    @balance += amount  @balance = balance + amount
  end
end
 
```

Now the object can DO things.

That’s real object-oriented programming.

OOP (Object Oriented Programming) - My Definition - OOP means creating classes that allow objects to be organized , manipulated, and gives functionality? - CLEANER VERSION - OOP is a programming style where code is organized around objects/classes that store data and behavior together. - EVEN SIMPLER - OOP MODELS real-world things as objects that have:
- data (attributes/state)
- functionality (methods/behavior)

